From: Brian Delwiche <delwiche@google.com>
Date: Wed, 24 Jun 2026 15:02:03 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 08/12] Fix cross-thread UAF in AvrcpService updates

A Use-After-Free (UAF) vulnerability exists in the Android Bluetooth AVRCP service due to a cross-thread race condition. Releasing the final `shared_ptr<Device>` reference on a non-owning thread (e.g., the Java MainLooper thread) could race against a queued `WeakPtr` task executing on the `bt_main_thread` during a disconnection, leading to potential memory corruption.

This commit resolves the issue by posting the device iteration via `do_in_main_thread` in `SendMediaUpdate`, `SendFolderUpdate`, and `SendPlayerSettingsChanged`. This ensures that the temporary vector returned by `GetListOfDevices()`, which holds `shared_ptr<Device>` references, is allocated and destroyed strictly on the `bt_main_thread`. The `shared_ptr` lifecycle remains single-threaded, enforcing the libchrome `WeakPtr` thread-safety contract. Additionally, the `instance_` and `connection_handler_` null checks are moved inside the lambdas to safely evaluate them on the execution thread.

Test: atest net_test_avrcp --host

Bug: 522373202
Flag: EXEMPT BUGFIX
This was autogenerated by MendIt (go/androidmendit)

